19:A buyer of computation often cannot see the quality of delivered work and can afford to check only a sample of it. We study a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, and free identity replacement, in a mechanism class without entry fees, identity certification, or collateral before receipts; a failed audit refunds the job's escrow and bans the serving identity for a finite number of rounds. Under uniform auditing, immediate free replacement makes the ban costless. The rule we study, R3, checks every job of a new identity while the round's audits allow it, and after that window keeps targeting the newest identities, so a replacement can stay under heavy inspection: persistent age-ranked targeting rather than release by age. We report two registered tests. In the first, the original claim failed against a sealed attacker family, and an amended claim, registered after development results were seen, passed on the public identity-replacement family, with interval procedures admitted by proxy. The second test compares R3 with the best of four tested adaptations of verified-history targeting at identical budget, payment, tolerance, ban, pacing, and sanctions, decomposes R3 into its three parts, adds stronger attacks, and repeats the first test's comparison on six market instances drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block.
35:\paragraph{Two registered tests.} Both tests used staged registration: a protocol locked and timestamped before the evaluated outcomes existed, and changes recorded in signed, timestamped amendments. The first test (October \const{2026}) locked its claim before the rule was implemented; development results then informed an amended claim and a successor rule, and both claims are reported. The second test (protocol v2) was locked with a Bitcoin timestamp before six fresh market instances and their seeds were drawn from a later block; it adds a matched comparator, a factorial decomposition, stronger attackers, and a replication of the first test's comparison.
49:\paragraph{Verifiable computation.} Proofs that a computation was executed as committed now reach large language models. FlashProve proves, after the fact, that a committed integer version of a 400-billion-parameter model produced a token as its greedy choice; its Lean proofs cover the protocol, while the executable prover and verifier were generated by AI and are not proved equivalent to it, and its technical report states that it was AI-generated and not reviewed in detail~\cite{rinberg2026flashprove}. Such proofs check that a committed computation ran; they do not check the quality of an output, which is what our audits observe.
54:\paragraph{Market.} Time is discrete, $t = 1, \dots, T$, with $T = 200$ in both tests except the first test's language-model attacker ($T = 100$). In each round $J = 50$ unit jobs arrive; job $j$ has value $v_j$ per unit of delivered quality, drawn independently and uniformly on $[v_{\min}, v_{\max}]$. There are $N = 10$ providers; provider $i$ has a quality ceiling $q^{\max}_i$, a per-job cost $a_i + b_i q$ at effort $q$, a capacity of $k_i$ jobs per round, and an outside option of zero. The provider population and the market parameters of each test are stated in Sections~\ref{sec:eval1} and~\ref{sec:design}.
129:\paragraph{Search quality.} Scheduled cells get a doubling check: the registered search continues with as many restarts again, and the cell is flagged if the doubled search improves the attacker's normalised profit or the reference's normalised profit (profit cells), the welfare attack's efficiency (welfare cells), or the coalition objective by at least \const{0.005}, or if a W1 search finds a feasible candidate where the original found none. A doubled search's winner is reported beside the cell and never replaces its frozen estimate. Cells without a scheduled check carry no search-quality claim. A cell is \emph{missing} if it did not complete or is invalid: not run (a cap stop or a cut), a refused sample, a nonfinite statistic, a search with no finite candidate, or a failed load of H4; a missing cell removes every decision that uses it. A \emph{search-flagged} cell keeps its decision, labelled as a decision about the prescribed search procedure, and leaves that rule's robustness statement for that family unanswered. The search bounds exposure to the searched families and optimiser; it does not certify robustness.
153:\paragraph{Result.} The original claim failed against H2: R3 passed the rule clause, but neither audited baseline met either baseline criterion (Table~\ref{tab:verdict1}). The amended claim passed: under D2+H3 the mean gains were \evone{0.570} for B2, \evone{0.353} for B3, and \evone{0.071} for R3. These are normalised excess focal profits, not losses of honest surplus; and the first report's sentence that R3 matched B3 and B2 under H2 ``within noise'' was unsupported, since no equivalence test was run (published erratum).
159:\section{The second test: design and registration}\label{sec:design}
164:\paragraph{Lock and draw.} The protocol file binds the code commit, the rules, families, tuned files, manifest, decision rules, predictions, budget, and calibration multipliers. It was signed and tagged \texttt{protocol-v2}, stamped with OpenTimestamps, and pushed with its upgraded proof when the Bitcoin tip was at block \const{974379}. The earliest attestation of the proof is at block $A = \const{974307}$; the draw block is $H = A + \const{144} = \const{974451}$, read once both explorers reported at least six blocks above it with the same hash. Had the push come at or after $H$, the draw would have been kept and its results labelled nonconfirmatory. We assume that the chain both explorers served at that read is the accepted chain and that no miner chose the block at $H$ to influence this evaluation; a later reorganisation deeper than six blocks is not handled and would be reported.
174:Q4 & Per market, an intersection-union test of $g_{F_{000}} - g_{F_{111}} > \const{0.10}$ under D2+H3 and $F_{111}$'s gross efficiency $> \const{0.90}$; the market's $p$-value is the larger of the two calibrated component $p$-values; Holm over the six markets; reported as $k$ of six, with failing markets named and undecided markets marked ``generalisation not established''. \\
182:\paragraph{Deviations from the protocol.} Two arose during the evaluation. First, the runner recalibrates each fresh market where it runs, and the evaluation ran on Linux x86 machines, while the registered instance file holds the calibration computed on the author's arm64 machine. In each fresh market, between \const{1} and \const{6} provider parameters differ between the two in the last binary digit (one unit in the last place, a relative difference near $10^{\const{-16}}$); the headline market is unaffected. The runner's verification compares configuration hashes exactly and refuses these records. We kept them: a container on the evaluation platform reproduces each run's configuration hash, every differing value is within one unit in the last place of the registered one, and verification passes on every other check when given the reproduced configuration. The check is a script in the artifact. It establishes that the configurations match, not that the decisions would be unchanged under the registered ones; that is not verified, because small input differences can change discrete simulation branches. The report's addendum lists the hashes, states the interval labels, and traces search-flag dependencies through each cell's references, which qualifies three further decisions as about the prescribed search procedure. Second, the fresh-market runs cost about ten percent more than estimated, and the author raised the evaluation cap from \const{40} to \const{43} USD and the written authorisation from \const{52} to \const{55} USD so that every registered cell could run; no analysis changed.
186:All values below come from the report that the tagged code wrote; the addendum beside it (Section~\ref{sec:design}) adds labels and disclosures and changes no estimate or decision. Gains are normalised attacker gains; intervals are those of Table~\ref{tab:rules-v2}.
188:\paragraph{Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1).} Pooled over the six fresh markets, C$^*$ leaves the identity-replacement attacker a gain higher than R3's by \site{r_q1_d_est} under D2+H3 and by \site{r_q1_x_est} under X7-SC. Holm's procedure rejects $d = \const{0}$ (D2+H3: $p = $ \site{r_q1_d_p}), so the sign is established, but both intervals lie inside the equivalence margin: the categories are ``\site{r_q1_d_cat}'' (D2+H3) and ``\site{r_q1_x_cat}'' (X7-SC), and the efficiency label is ``\site{r_q1_d_eff}''. R3 is not materially better than the best tested adaptation of verified-history targeting. The difference changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from \site{r_q1_f3_d} on fresh3 to \site{r_q1_f1_d} on fresh1 under D2+H3. Both decisions are about the prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test uses them through its references (addendum). The side evidence (Table~\ref{tab:q1-side}) shows near-equal efficiency, audit spending, refunds, and served share; the two rules differ mainly in the attacker's truthful reference profit.
205:\paragraph{Repeating the comparison on six new markets (Q4).} The first test's comparison, B3 against R3 under D2+H3 with R3's efficiency condition, replicates on \site{r_q4_k} of the six markets (Table~\ref{tab:q4}). On every market R3 leaves a lower gain than B3 and keeps its efficiency condition; where the replication fails, the gain difference is smaller than the registered threshold, not reversed: \site{r_q4_f0} on fresh0, \site{r_q4_f2} on fresh2, and \site{r_q4_f5} on fresh5, against \site{r_q4_f1} on fresh1. R3's efficiency on fresh2, the lowest, is \site{r_q4_f2_eff}. The passing decisions on fresh1, fresh3, and fresh4 are about the prescribed search procedure (fresh3 through its references; addendum). The registered prediction of at least five passing markets failed. Generalisation of the full first-test claim to this neighbourhood is therefore not established.
258:\paragraph{Search quality.} No cell is missing. The search-flagged cells, their consequences, and the dependencies traced through references are listed in the report and its addendum; each affected statement above names its label.
260:\paragraph{Predictions.} Table~\ref{tab:predictions} scores every registered prediction from the report. The failures are informative: we expected the priority phase to matter little, the replication to hold on at least five markets, X5-AG/v2 to stay below D2+H3, and the unconstrained welfare attack to leave R3's and C$^*$'s efficiency at the registered level, and none of these held.
264:\caption{Registered predictions, scored from the report.}\label{tab:predictions}
270:\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} Against the identity-replacement attacker, targeting new identities lowers the gain that free replacement buys: R3 leaves a lower gain than uniform auditing on every sampled market, with its efficiency condition met. The margin the first test claimed does not carry over to the whole neighbourhood, and the advantage is not specific to R3: a rule that ranks identities by verified history does about as well, and in the factorial either the priority phase or newest-first targeting alone recovers most of the effect. The finding is therefore about age- or history-ranked scrutiny of new identities as a class, not about R3's particular design. Pacing contributes little at the studied load, and the terminal reserve that pacing adds is the source of R3's collapse at high load, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
283:\paragraph{Availability.} The public release at the report commit contains the code, the tuned files, all records of both tests (including incomplete, failed, and cut cells), the market generator, the block headers, the paper sources, and a one-command reproduction of every table.
