\section{The second test: design and registration}\label{sec:design}
\paragraph{Questions.} Q1: does R3 ($F_{111}$) leave a lower or higher identity-replacement gain than C$^*$, at the fixed pair? Q2: what do the priority phase, the residual targeting, and the pacing contribute, alone and together? Q3: do the results hold against X7-SC, X5-AG/v2, the defence-aware sealed family H4, welfare attacks, and coalitions, and does R4 repair the reserve collapse? Q4: does the first test's comparison of B3 and R3 under D2+H3 replicate on fresh markets? Q1 and Q4 are confirmatory; Q2 and Q3 are descriptive.

\paragraph{Development.} All choices before the draw were development, on the headline market and the development instance dev0 (branch \texttt{two20k}): a benchmark priced every cell; the comparator was selected and the tuned variants were tuned; the defenders were frozen at a signed commit; a separate session wrote H4 against the frozen defenders and committed only its hash; a coverage check on frozen development attacks admitted calibration multipliers $\kappa = \const{0.5}$ for Q1 and $\kappa = \const{0.25}$ for Q4; and the predictions were written and hashed. Budget caps were \const{12} USD for development and \const{40} USD for evaluation, within a written authorisation of \const{52} USD. The evaluation cap forced three registered cuts: the tuned variants, H4, and the D2 extras run on the headline market only.

\paragraph{Lock and draw.} The protocol file binds the code commit, the rules, families, tuned files, manifest, decision rules, predictions, budget, and calibration multipliers. It was signed and tagged \texttt{protocol-v2}, stamped with OpenTimestamps, and pushed with its upgraded proof when the Bitcoin tip was at block \const{974379}. The earliest attestation of the proof is at block $A = \const{974307}$; the draw block is $H = A + \const{144} = \const{974451}$, read once both explorers reported at least six blocks above it with the same hash. Had the push come at or after $H$, the draw would have been kept and its results labelled nonconfirmatory. We assume that the chain both explorers served at that read is the accepted chain and that no miner chose the block at $H$ to influence this evaluation; a later reorganisation deeper than six blocks is not handled and would be reported.

\paragraph{Fresh markets.} The block hash seeds a generator of market instances around the headline market: fixed scale, noise, budget ratio, audit cost, and cost-quality association, with provider ceilings, costs, capacities, and the top job value drawn per instance (Appendix~\ref{app:params}). Six instances and 200 evaluation seeds per market were drawn from it. The claims concern this neighbourhood, not markets in general.

\paragraph{Decision rules.} Table~\ref{tab:rules-v2} summarises them; the protocol file is the authority. Pooled statistics weight the six markets equally, and intervals use a studentized bootstrap that resamples seeds within each market.

\begin{table}[t]\centering\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{\const{0.78}\linewidth}@{}}\toprule
Question & Rule \\\midrule
Q1 & $d = g_{C^*} - g_{F_{111}}$, pooled, for D2+H3 and X7-SC. Direction: Holm's procedure~\cite{holm1979simple} on calibrated two-sided $p$-values for $d = \const{0}$. Size: the calibrated Bonferroni interval $I$ for $d$. Categories, in order: \emph{materially lower} if $I$ lies above $\const{0.05}$ (R3 lower) or below $\const{-0.05}$ (C$^*$ lower); \emph{equivalent} if $I$ lies within $[\const{-0.05}, \const{0.05}]$, with a direction label when Holm rejects $d = \const{0}$; \emph{ordered, size not established} if Holm rejects and neither applies; otherwise \emph{inconclusive}. A rule is called better only with the first category in its favour and a paired gross-efficiency difference (that rule minus the other) whose nominal one-sided \const{97.5}\% lower bound is at least $\const{-0.01}$ (descriptive; no calibrated joint guarantee). If that lower bound is below $\const{-0.01}$, the gain category stands and the result is labelled ``efficiency non-inferiority unresolved''; it is labelled ``lower gain with an efficiency loss'' only if the corresponding upper bound is below $\const{-0.01}$. \\
Q4 & Per market, an intersection-union test of $g_{F_{000}} - g_{F_{111}} > \const{0.10}$ under D2+H3 and $F_{111}$'s gross efficiency $> \const{0.90}$; the market's $p$-value is the larger of the two calibrated component $p$-values; Holm over the six markets; reported as $k$ of six, with failing markets named and undecided markets marked ``generalisation not established''. \\
Q2, Q3 & Descriptive, with nominal intervals; every extremum selected on training data. \\\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
