129:\paragraph{Search quality.} Scheduled cells get a doubling check: the registered search continues with as many restarts again, and the cell is flagged if the doubled search improves the attacker's normalised profit or the reference's normalised profit (profit cells), the welfare attack's efficiency (welfare cells), or the coalition objective by at least \const{0.005}, or if a W1 search finds a feasible candidate where the original found none. A doubled search's winner is reported beside the cell and never replaces its frozen estimate. Cells without a scheduled check carry no search-quality claim. A cell is \emph{missing} if it did not complete or is invalid: not run (a cap stop or a cut), a refused sample, a nonfinite statistic, a search with no finite candidate, or a failed load of H4; a missing cell removes every decision that uses it. A \emph{search-flagged} cell keeps its decision, labelled as a decision about the prescribed search procedure, and leaves that rule's robustness statement for that family unanswered. The search bounds exposure to the searched families and optimiser; it does not certify robustness.
217:\paragraph{Which parts of R3 matter (Q2).} Under separately searched D2+H3 attacks, pooled over the fresh markets, the priority phase P lowers the gain by \site{r_q2_p} and newest-first residual targeting G by \site{r_q2_g} (main effects), while pacing S changes it by \site{r_q2_s}. The two targeting parts are substitutes: their interaction is \site{r_q2_pg}, and G changes the gain by \site{r_q2_g_p0} without the priority phase but by only \site{r_q2_g_p1} with it. Either form of age-ranked targeting removes most of the replacement gain; adding the other adds little. Fixed attacks give the same picture (P: \site{r_q2_fixed_p}), so the effect is not an artefact of the search. Our registered conjectures (P negligible, the targeting parts additive, S helpful) failed (Table~\ref{tab:predictions}). X7-SC agrees (Table~\ref{tab:q2}).
234:\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope, the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. Against B3 the envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it stays at \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ at \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. The independently authored, defence-aware family H4, run at the headline market only, gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness: the training-selected attack without an attacker-profit constraint (W0) lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{r_q3_w0}, as it does for the other core rules, while with the constraint (W1) efficiency stays at \site{r_q3_w1} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). Coalitions gain far more against B3 (two synchronised members: \site{r_q3_coal_f000}) than against R3 (five synchronised members: \site{r_q3_coal_f111}); we make no coalition-stability claim (Table~\ref{tab:q3-coalitions}). At $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves \site{r_rep_f111}; the repair R4 lowers this to \site{r_rep_r4} and C$^*$ with the capped schedule (C$^*$/S2) to \site{r_rep_cs2} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-repair}).
258:\paragraph{Search quality.} No cell is missing. The search-flagged cells, their consequences, and the dependencies traced through references are listed in the report and its addendum; each affected statement above names its label.
260:\paragraph{Predictions.} Table~\ref{tab:predictions} scores every registered prediction from the report. The failures are informative: we expected the priority phase to matter little, the replication to hold on at least five markets, X5-AG/v2 to stay below D2+H3, and the unconstrained welfare attack to leave R3's and C$^*$'s efficiency at the registered level, and none of these held.
270:\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} Against the identity-replacement attacker, targeting new identities lowers the gain that free replacement buys: R3 leaves a lower gain than uniform auditing on every sampled market, with its efficiency condition met. The margin the first test claimed does not carry over to the whole neighbourhood, and the advantage is not specific to R3: a rule that ranks identities by verified history does about as well, and in the factorial either the priority phase or newest-first targeting alone recovers most of the effect. The finding is therefore about age- or history-ranked scrutiny of new identities as a class, not about R3's particular design. Pacing contributes little at the studied load, and the terminal reserve that pacing adds is the source of R3's collapse at high load, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
280:Free identity replacement makes a ban costless under uniform audits, and directing audits at new or unverified identities restores much of its force. Two registered tests, the second on fresh markets drawn after the lock, show that this holds in sign across the sampled markets but not with the margin first claimed, that a verified-history rule matches the age-ranked rule we proposed, and that neither protects efficiency against loss-accepting welfare attacks. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, is public.

